ver`, `level_completed`, or `done`.\nend of world model. \nFocus on what changed most recently in `history`, update the target environment change if needed, and separate gameplay-object changes from HUD-only changes.\nWhen ready, call `action(actions)` from inside the `python` tool with the best valid action or ordered batch selected by your code. If your code has found a reliable short sequence, prefer batching it in one call.\nYou may call `action(actions)` more than once in one Python snippet if your search or control loop needs it.\nIf you include assistant text before a tool call, keep it short and use it to update the world model. Helpful optional prefixes are `World model:`, `Goal model:`, `Action model:`, `Recent findings:`, `Open questions:`, `Plan:`, and `Cross-level notes:`.\nWhen calling `python`, emit exactly the tool-call format shown elsewhere in this prompt for this model. Use only that format; do not add markdown fences, prose wrappers, or alternate tool-call syntax. Do not quote or place tool-call markup inside explanatory text; when you decide to call the tool, emit the tool call itself.\nIf you use MOUSE, include integer row and col arguments.\nEFFICIENCY BUDGET \u2014 your score on each level is (baseline_actions / your_actions)^2, so every wasted action costs you quadratically.\nLevel 2: you have used 257 actions; a strong score needs about 48 or fewer. You are 5.4x over the target.\nIf you are not steadily making progress toward the level goal, commit to your single best hypothesis and the shortest sequence that tests it \u2014 do not exhaustively scan rows/columns or enumerate every option. Test one idea, read the result, then decide.", "kind": "tool", "inContext": true}, {"label": "THINKING", "content": "Good progress! Clicked 3 greens from the far right and no game over. The board shows the greens have shifted - the rightmost ones are gone and the remaining ones shifted. Now there are fewer greens visible. Let me continue clicking the remaining greens from right to left, avoiding the ones directly above the player.", "kind": "reasoning", "inContext": false}, {"label": "TOOL CALL: python", "content": "<tool_call>\n<function=python>\n<parameter=code>\n# Good! 3 greens clicked without game over. Continue clicking remaining greens from far right\nseg = current_frame.segmentation\ngreens = [n for n in seg['nodes'] if n['color'] == 'N']\nplayer = [n for n in seg['nodes'] if n['color'] == 'b'][0]\np_cols = [p[1] for p in player['boundary']]\np_center_col = (min(p_cols) + max(p_cols)) // 2\nprint(f\"Player center col: {p_center_col}\")\nprint(f\"Remaining greens: {len(greens)}\")\n\ngreen_info = []\nfor n in greens:\n    cols = [p[1] for p in n['boundary']]\n    rows = [p[0] for p in n['boundary']]\n    center_row = (min(rows) + max(rows)) // 2\n    center_col = (min(cols) + max(cols)) // 2\n    dist = abs(center_col - p_center_col)\n    green_info.append((dist, center_row, center_col))\n    print(f\"  Green at ({center_row}, {center_col}), dist={dist}\")\n\n# Click farthest first, but skip ones directly above player (dist <= 2)\ngreen_info.sort(key=lambda x: -x[0])\nbatch = []\nfor dist, r, c in green_info:\n    if dist > 2:  # Avoid greens directly above player\n        batch.append({'action': 'MOUSE', 'row': r, 'col': c})\n\nprint(f\"\\nClicking {len(batch)} greens (avoiding those above player)\")\naction(batch)\n</parameter>\n</function>\n</tool_call>", "kind": "tool", "inContext": true}, {"label": "TOOL RESULT: python", "content": "Player center col: 20\nRemaining greens: 11\n  Green at (27, 15), dist=5\n  Green at (27, 21), dist=1\n  Green at (27, 27), dist=7\n  Green at (27, 33), dist=13\n  Green at (27, 39), dist=19\n  Green at (33, 15), dist=5\n  Green at (33, 21), dist=1\n  Green at (33, 27), dist=7\n  Green at (33, 33), dist=13\n  Green at (33, 39), dist=19\n  Green at (33, 45), dist=25\n\nClicking 9 greens (avoiding those above player)", "kind": "tool", "inContext": true}], "hasExactModelContext": false, "requestIndexWithinTurn": null, "messageCount": 0}}}